  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Science careers that involve travel in India are not one job — they are a filter across six
    genuinely different fields:</strong> geology and mining/petroleum exploration, environmental and EIA
    fieldwork, marine biology and oceanography, wildlife biology and conservation, science journalism, and
    the rare edge case of polar or test-range science. Building a real <strong>high-value skill
    portfolio</strong> inside the right one of these, not chasing "travel" as a job title, is what actually
    opens higher income opportunities and earlier financial freedom here.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"Science careers that involve travel" is a cross-cutting angle, not a single profession — it sits across six established fields, each with its own degree, entrance route, and pay scale.</li>
      <li>Most of the real travel here is domestic, occasional, and site- or season-based, not constant international movement. Say that plainly before choosing a field for the travel alone.</li>
      <li>Geology/mining exploration and environmental/EIA consulting carry the strongest realistic pay ceilings, including a genuine independent-consulting ownership path.</li>
      <li>Wildlife conservation fieldwork is the most travel- and field-heavy, and typically the lowest-paying and least stable.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick the underlying science first. The travel is a real feature of these fields, not a reason to choose one on its own.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

