          <span>{item.watch}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="geology">Geology and mining/petroleum exploration fieldwork</h2>

  <p>
    This is the strongest travel-plus-ceiling combination of the six. The Geological Survey of India (GSI)
    conducts nationwide geological mapping fieldwork and recruits geologists through its own or a
    UPSC-linked combined geo-scientist exam. ONGC (Oil and Natural Gas Corporation) hires exploration
    geologists and geophysicists for onshore and offshore fieldwork, drilling-site postings, and seismic
    surveys, largely through GATE scores in Geology, Geophysics, or Petroleum Engineering followed by an
    interview. Private mining and metals companies, including Coal India, Vedanta, Hindustan Zinc, and
    NMDC, run their own exploration-geologist fieldwork on domestic mine sites.
  </p>

  <p>
    A junior exploration geologist can expect field seasons of several weeks to a few months at a stretch,
    at a remote site or, for ONGC roles, on an offshore rig for defined rotation periods. This is real,
    physically demanding, rural or offshore fieldwork, not a desk job with occasional site visits, and it
    is almost entirely domestic.
  </p>

  <h2 id="environmental">Environmental and EIA fieldwork</h2>

  <p>
    Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) consulting is fieldwork-heavy by legal requirement: every major
    infrastructure, mining, or industrial project in India needs baseline environmental data, air, water,
    soil, and noise, collected on-site before a clearance from the Ministry of Environment, Forest and
    Climate Change or a State Environment Impact Assessment Authority. That means real, recurring site
    visits for the consultants who collect and verify this data.
  </p>

  <p>
    This track already has a full, dedicated breakdown on this site, including every pay stage from junior
    associate to independent consultant, because it is a real career in its own right, not just a travel
    angle. Read the complete picture in our
    <a href="/blog/career-options/environmental-science-career-options">environmental science career
    options</a> guide; the honest summary for this article is that the travel is short and repeated rather
    than long-postings-based, and the ceiling above it is genuinely ownable through independent consulting.
  </p>

  <h2 id="marine">Marine biology and oceanography</h2>

  <p>
    India's marine-science research runs mainly through the National Institute of Oceanography (NIO, a CSIR
    lab based in Goa) and the Central Marine Fisheries Research Institute (CMFRI). Entry typically follows
    the same JRF-to-scientist ladder common to Indian pure-science research: clear CSIR-UGC NET, a
    DBT/ICMR-JRF exam, or GATE to access a funded fellowship (roughly Rs 37,000-42,000 a month, not a
    salary, capped at five years), then compete separately, later, for a permanent Scientist-grade post.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here is the honest correction most people need: most working marine biologists in India spend far more
    time in coastal labs and field stations than at sea. Research-vessel cruises happen, but they are
    occasional, tied to specific funded projects, and berths are limited and assigned by need, not a routine
    feature of the job. If your interest is closer to this specific science than to environmental fieldwork
    or wildlife conservation, our
    <a href="/blog/career-options/life-sciences-and-biological-sciences-career-options">biological sciences
    career options</a> guide covers the wider life-sciences research ladder in depth, including the
    government-scientist pay stages this track shares.
  </p>

  <h2 id="wildlife">Wildlife biology and conservation</h2>

  <p>
    This is the most genuinely fieldwork-saturated track on this list, and typically the lowest-paying. Real
    roles exist at the Wildlife Institute of India (WII), WWF-India, the Wildlife Trust of India (WTI), and
    the Bombay Natural History Society (BNHS), doing species surveys, camera-trap monitoring, habitat
    assessment, and community-conservation work that genuinely means living near or inside a reserve or
    field station for extended stretches, not short site visits.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most of these roles are project-funded rather than permanent, which means real income instability
    across years, not just at the start. A forest-department route, Indian Forest Service through UPSC, or
    a state Forest Range Officer post through a state PSC exam, offers more stable, government-scale pay for
    fieldwork-heavy conservation and enforcement work, but it runs through its own competitive selection
    process separate from the research-institute route.
  </p>

  <h2 id="journalism">Science journalism and communication</h2>

  <p>
    The one track here where the ceiling is not set by an institution's pay scale, but by whether you build
    a specific, trusted niche. A staff science reporter's travel is assignment-based: covering an ISRO
    launch at Sriharikota, a scientific conference, or a field story on climate or wildlife, occasional and
    tied to beat seniority, not a routine feature of an entry-level role.
  </p>

  <p>
    The real scaling move here is different from the other five tracks: an independent science writer or
    creator who deliberately builds a reputation around one fieldwork specialty, glaciers, deep-sea
    research, wildlife conservation, can turn that into a genuinely scalable content or consulting business
    over years, earning trust (and eventually paid access) with the researchers and institutions doing the
    fieldwork. This is real, but it is built, not handed out with the first job.
  </p>

  <h2 id="niche-tracks">Polar expeditions and test-range science: the rare edge cases</h2>

  <p>
    These come up constantly in "travel science career" searches, and they deserve an honest, direct answer
    rather than being quietly left out.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>India's Antarctic and Arctic research programme, run through the National Centre for Polar and
      Ocean Research (NCPOR), sends a genuinely small number of scientists a year</strong>, almost always
      PhD-level specialists in glaciology, atmospheric science, or polar oceanography, to stations like
      Maitri and Bharati. This is not an entry-level outcome and never was; it is a rare deployment inside an
      already-advanced research career, reached after years of specialisation, not a job you apply for out
      of a bachelor's degree.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Test-range science, flight-test and trial-campaign work at DRDO, ISRO, or HAL, involves real travel to
    test sites like Chandipur, Sriharikota, or Thumba during trial campaigns. It is genuine fieldwork, but it
    is domestic, campaign-based, and reached only through a competitive DRDO/ISRO scientist-entry exam and
    interview, typically on top of an engineering or physics-heavy degree, not a general-science route.
  </p>

  <p>
    If either of these genuinely fits your interest, the honest plan is to build the underlying
    specialisation, glaciology, atmospheric science, aerospace or defence engineering, and treat the
    expedition or trial-site travel as a possible bonus of a strong research or engineering career, not the
    reason to choose the degree in the first place.
  </p>

